The tests look reasonable. Now let me review the remaining diff portion of test_smAgent.js (the rest we saw in pr_diff.txt tail: fail_validation latched, conflict_rework latched, no statePublish). Those were in the truncated diff tail — I saw them.

Now let me think carefully about deeper design/correctness issues.

### Deep issue 1: `consultReworkLatch` probes head runs only when an entry exists; arm happens without probing. Consider the ordering race in the SAME tick that arms:

Tick T: arm_rework arms (pr,head) → latch map armed with at=T. Later in the SAME tick, another rule (fail_validation) for the same PR consults → entry present, fresh → probe → maybe no run yet (dispatch takes time; label was just added; the rework-on-label dispatch may have already happened earlier in the tick — actually arm_rework only adds the label; the dispatch is a separate rule). Suppressed. Good.

### Deep issue 2: latch armed in conflict_rework path BEFORE checking `armed` for the LABEL path (cLinkedOpen case):

```js
if (cIsMachinePr) {
    if (cLinked && cLinkedOpen) {
        if (!DRY) {
            github_add_labels({...cLinked, agent:rework});
        }
    } else {
        armed = DRY || triggerWorkflow(...);
    }
if (!armed) { ...defer...; continue; }
armReworkLatch(ticket);
```

For the linked-issue path: `github_add_labels` is NOT wrapped in try/catch here. If it throws, the exception propagates to the outer catch (`❌ conflict_rework failed`) — armReworkLatch never runs. OK — consistent (no arm without label).

For the PR-anchored path: armed = triggerWorkflow() === true. triggerWorkflow returns false on workflow cap / active run / dispatch failure. So arm only after dispatch truly landed. Good.

BUT: in the linked-issue path, `armed` stays `true` even if `github_add_labels` silently fails? It throws → outer catch. OK.

Hmm wait, one thing: in the linked-issue path, `armReworkLatch(ticket)` is called even when `DRY` is false and labels added — good. But what about the GUEST PR case (cIsMachinePr false)? `armed` stays true (initialized true) → armReworkLatch(ticket) arms the latch for a guest PR with NO leg dispatched. As analyzed — harmless because consult is machine-gated, but it does pollute the snapshot with phantom entries that live up to 45 min... Actually wait — do they get pruned? `pruneStale` prunes entries older than 45 min with no active run. A phantom guest entry with no run: at publish, evaluate → runs given → no leg run on head → age: fresh → 'in-flight' → KEPT. It stays in the snapshot until 45 min pass. Since consult is machine-gated, no suppression impact. But the conflict_rework marker scan `alreadyReported` is not latch-based. So impact = snapshot noise only. Still, it's a genuine wart worth flagging (IMPORTANT or SUGGESTION). The comment above the arm says "the PR-anchored dispatch landed" — but for guests no dispatch happened at all. The arm should be gated on `cIsMachinePr`. I'll flag as IMPORTANT (correctness of the latch semantics; no functional damage though since consumers gate on machine authorship... wait — do they? Let me double-check ALL consult sites are machine-gated:

1. arm_rework: `if (consultReworkLatch(...).latched) continue;` — NOT machine-gated in the diff! The arm_rework rule query has `prMachineAuthor: true`? In the test the rule includes `prMachineAuthor: true`. The rule's query filters machine-authored PRs. So the ticket reaching arm_rework is machine-authored by query. Hmm, but the latch consult there doesn't check isMachinePr — the query already gates. OK.

2. conflict_rework: gated by `cIsMachinePr &&`.

3. fail_validation: `reworkLatched = isMachinePr ? consult : false` — gated.

So guest-phantom latch entries never suppress anything. Fine — SUGGESTION level (snapshot hygiene + misleading comment). Actually, let me reconsider: could a guest-armed entry suppress a FUTURE machine arm for the same (pr, head)? PRs don't change authorship. No. OK, SUGGESTION.

### Deep issue 3: fail_validation — the latch suppression suppresses the LABEL add but the red report still posts (per test "report still posts, rework re-arm suppressed"). Is that per AC1? AC1 says the armer logs ⏭️ and dispatches nothing. Posting the report is fine.

But WAIT — there's an interplay bug candidate: in fail_validation, when `reworkLatched` is true, the code SKIPS the `github_add_labels` for the linked issue... but does it also skip the `ai_validating` label removal and the red report? No — those happen before. Good.

Hmm, but here's a subtle one: in fail_validation the suppression prevents the empty-lap counter from incrementing? The empty-lap markers are written... where? `redLine = redHeadMarkerLine(priorReds, failHead, redCap)` — is a marker line included in the report; recording of redHeads is done by... let me check if there's a separate marker write (github_create_comment with marker). The report includes the markers — the next failMarkerState parses PR comments. Since the report still posts while latched, red-head counting still advances each red while a leg flies. Could that pre-cap the PR (redHeadCap default 3) while the latch suppresses arms for 45 min? Scenario: leg in flight, head goes red repeatedly (validation re-runs on the same head — each red tick increments priorReds for the SAME head). redHeadCap=3: the validate_pr arm side refuses to re-arm a head that already went red 3 times. But wait — that's for REVALIDATION arming, not rework. Hmm, this could mark the head as capped while the leg is working. Is that new behavior from this PR? No — that marker posting happens on every red regardless (pre-existing), the latch only skips the agent:rework label add. Actually wait, pre-PR behavior: red → report + label add each tick (the storm). Post-PR: red → report (same) but no label. The red-head marker still lands identically. So no change there. Fine.

### Deep issue 4: `reworkLatchStateCfg` vs publish gating mismatch — publish requires `spCfgRaw.channel === 'release' && !DRY`. Latch load requires `channel === 'release'` (no DRY check — but consult in DRY still loads prev state — fine, read-only).

BUT: the publish repo fallback: `spRepo = cfg.repo ? parse : effectiveRepoInfo-based`. Latch load mirrors it. Consistent.

### Deep issue 5 — **the publish path runs `fetchPreviousState` TWICE per tick now** (once in `tickReworkLatchState` lazily at first consult/arm, once in the publish block for `spPrev`). Two extra gh API calls per tick (content GET). Minor perf cost, consistent with the codebase's style of extra probes. Could pass the prev through... minor. Also NOTE: the two fetches could race (state changes between) — negligible.

Actually wait — there's something subtler. The publish block passes `prev: spPrev` and `reworkInFlight: tickReworkLatch.map`. buildFactoryState merges prev.reworkInFlight + live map. So the double fetch is required for the merge. OK.

### Deep issue 6 — **`buildFactoryState` prune uses `runs` filtered how?** `input.runs` here is `spRunList` = `github_list_workflow_runs(perPage: 50)` — the 50 newest runs repo-wide, ALL workflows. latchEffectOfRun checks `run.head_sha` vs entry head. Since the list is repo-wide newest-50, a leg run on an old head may have fallen off → age-based fallback. Acceptable, documented? Not explicitly, but fine.

BUT — hmm, `evaluate` with runs given: consider TWO legs' runs on the same head — one old completed BEFORE the arm, one active. Loop: first matching effect wins? Order: for each run, if effect 'in-flight' return immediately; if 'terminated' → also return immediately! Wait:

```js
for (var i = 0; i < runs.length; i++) {
    var effect = latchEffectOfRun(runs[i], entry.head, atMs, workflowFile);
    if (effect === 'in-flight') return 'in-flight';
    if (effect === 'terminated') return 'terminated';
}
```

If the FIRST run in the list is an old TERMINATED leg run (completed before the arm — no wait, latchEffectOfRun returns 'terminated' only when endedMs >= entryAtMs). A run completed before the arm returns null (the `endedMs >= entryAtMs` check fails → null). So 'terminated' is only returned for runs that concluded AT/AFTER the arm. Could there be BOTH an in-flight run AND a concluded-after-arm run? The leg workflow one head: run A completed after arm, run B queued (a second dispatch — the very storm we're preventing). If run A (terminated) comes first in the list → latch cleared → re-arm while run B still queued → duplicate leg! Runs are newest-first from GitHub API... The newest run B (active) would come first typically. Given GitHub returns newest-first, 'in-flight' is checked first in practice. Edge-case order dependency — very unlikely; both dispatches for the same (pr,head) are exactly what the latch prevents going forward. I'd note it as a nit/suggestion at most. Actually, this could matter in the storm replay scenario: pre-latch legs already stacked — run A completed (the one working leg), run B... hmm no, 8 dead legs never produced runs. OK, negligible — I'll mention as a defensive ordering note (suggestion-level).

### Deep issue 7 — AC2 test: "leg run CONCLUDED after the arm → latch cleared, re-arm proceeds" — but WHO re-arms? The consult clears the latch AND the arming path proceeds to arm in the same tick. Good — dead-letter recovery.

### Deep issue 8 — **`hasActiveLegRun` in smAgent has a stale-zombie guard (`isStaleNonRunningWorkflowRun`) that `reworkLatch.isLegRun`/ACTIVE_RUN_STATES lacks.** The latch's run-based keep-alive ("a queued run on a jammed runner must not invite a duplicate") does NOT apply the stale-zombie window: a leg run stuck 'queued' for HOURS keeps the latch alive indefinitely (evaluate: any active-status run → 'in-flight', regardless of age — the staleMs check is skipped when a run is in-flight... wait no:

```js
if (runs) { for ... if 'in-flight' return 'in-flight'; }
if (nowMs - atMs > staleMsOf(opts)) return 'stale';
return 'in-flight';
```

An active run returns 'in-flight' BEFORE the age check → latch kept forever as long as a zombie queued run sits on the head. smAgent's own `hasActiveLegRun` ages out a queued run after a window (isStaleNonRunningWorkflowRun). The latch deliberately diverges — comment says "a queued run on a jammed runner must not invite a duplicate" — the OPPOSITE tradeoff from hasActiveLegRun which ages zombies out to allow update_branch. So: a zombie queued ai-teammate run (e.g., runner jam, or GitHub queue purge — does GitHub ever leave runs queued forever? Usually they eventually fail, but runner jams happen) would latch the rework armer indefinitely (beyond 45 min), requiring manual intervention — which contradicts AC3's "self-heal, no human" for the no-run case, but this is the run-exists-but-never-starts case. The ticket says: "a leg that never produced a run must be re-armable after a bounded timeout (not permanently latched)". A run stuck in 'queued' is arguably "no run" from the work perspective. The smAgent codebase already has `isStaleNonRunningWorkflowRun` for exactly this zombie shape (used in hasActiveLegRun). The latch not reusing it = potential permanent latch on a zombie queued run. Severity: IMPORTANT (real edge; contradicts AC3's spirit; the codebase has precedent for the guard). Let me look at isStaleNonRunningWorkflowRun to cite it.

